<div class="section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>What &ldquo;coherence&rdquo; actually means</h2>
    <p>Heart coherence isn't a metaphor. It's a specific, measurable pattern in heart-rate variability, defined in peer-reviewed research.</p>

    <h3>Heart Rate Variability: New Perspectives</h3>
    <p>A chaotic, irregular heart rhythm shows up as scattered energy spread across many frequencies. A coherent one compresses nearly all of that energy into a single, narrow, sine-wave-like pattern. This paper defines the coherence ratio used to tell the two apart.</p>
    <p class="meta">McCraty &amp; Shaffer, <em>Global Advances in Health and Medicine</em>, 2015. Peer-reviewed. <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4311559/">Read the study &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section section--tint">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Research on students under test pressure</h2>

    <h3>Emotion self-regulation and test anxiety</h3>
    <p>In an experiment measuring students' physiology directly, high schoolers who learned this technique were able to shift into a calmer state on demand, even right before a stressful test, and that calmer baseline held up over time, not just in the moment.</p>
    <p class="meta">Bradley, McCraty, Atkinson, et al., <em>Applied Psychophysiology and Biofeedback</em>, 2010. Peer-reviewed. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20559707/">Read the study &rarr;</a></p>

    <h3>The TestEdge National Demonstration Study</h3>
    <p>In a U.S. Department of Education&ndash;funded study of 10th-graders at two California high schools, test anxiety was common and closely tied to lower test performance. Students who went through the program saw a meaningful drop in test anxiety, along with better classroom experience and improved test performance compared to a control group.</p>
    <p class="meta">HeartMath Institute research report, with Claremont Graduate University. Not peer-reviewed (institute report). <a href="https://www.issuelab.org/resources/3089/3089.pdf">Read the report &rarr;</a></p>

    <h3>Self-regulation training in college freshmen</h3>
    <p>College freshmen who practiced this training, compared to freshmen doing high-intensity exercise and a no-treatment group, were the only ones to see less school burnout and test anxiety, along with better concentration and working memory.</p>
    <p class="meta">May, Seibert, Sanchez-Gonzalez &amp; Fincham, <em>Stress</em>, 2018. Peer-reviewed. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30345850/">Read the study &rarr;</a></p>

    <h3>Math-readiness training at a community college</h3>
    <p>Community-college students preparing for a math placement exam, taught this technique alongside their regular math instruction, showed steadily stronger readiness scores as the program became more fully integrated into the course.</p>
    <p class="meta">Vislocky &amp; Leslie, University of Cincinnati Clermont College. Not peer-reviewed (faculty report). <a href="https://www.heartmath.org/assets/uploads/site/file/pdf/e/efficacy-and-implementation-of-heartmath-instruction.pdf">Read the report &rarr;</a></p>

    <p class="meta">These are published studies of heart-coherence training. They aren't results from Third Mind Learning students.</p>
  </div>
</div>
